Ladybird measures a numeric tab-size in spaces of the first available
font of the tab’s nearest block container. Blink and Gecko do the same:
Blink’s InlineNode::FontForTab() takes the block’s font, and its
PrimaryFontForTabSize() picks the font that has a space glyph, whose
SpaceWidth() Font::TabWidthInternal() then counts. Gecko’s
ComputeTabWidthAppUnits() reads the containing block’s font metrics,
and counts the width of the first font valid for a space. WebKit
differs: FontCascade::tabWidth() counts the spaces of the font its
width iterator resolved for the tab’s own glyph. In a manual check,
Chrome and Firefox both agree with Ladybird’s new results, including
for a tab inside an inline box set in another font.
